\section{Method}

\subsection{Task Definition and Diagnostic Signal Metrics}

We study \emph{verdict prediction}: given a Python function, a test input, and an optional execution trace, predict whether the test passes or fails.
The model produces a single token (\texttt{pass} or \texttt{fail}), making this a binary classification task.
Unlike code generation \citep{chen2025tracepile} or output prediction \citep{gu2024cruxeval}, verdict prediction isolates the model's ability to detect faults from its ability to simulate execution, providing cleaner ablation contrasts than generation tasks.

To quantify signal quality without circular reference to model accuracy, we define the \emph{diagnostic token ratio} $d = n_{\text{diag}} / n_{\text{total}}$, where $n_{\text{diag}}$ counts tokens matching exception or assertion patterns.
This model-free metric correlates with accuracy at Pearson $r{=}0.949$ ($p{=}0.014$, $N{=}5$ main conditions), but captures only the \emph{explicit} diagnostic pathway; tail-truncated conditions achieve 93--97\% with $d {\approx} 0$, indicating a second, implicit signal channel operating through terminal execution state (\S\ref{sec:density}).

\subsection{Dataset}
\label{sec:dataset}

We sampled 10 candidate solutions per problem from Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct (temperature 0.8, top\_p 0.95) for 591 problems from HumanEval \citep{chen2021evaluating} (164) and MBPP-sanitized \citep{austin2021program} (427), executed each against canonical tests with \texttt{sys.settrace} to obtain line-level traces and pass/fail verdicts, yielding 5,910 pairs (67\% pass, 33\% fail).
The dataset is split 80/20 by problem ID into 4,720 training and 1,190 test samples (majority baseline: 71.51\%).
The \texttt{exception\_only} format collapses passing solutions into identical rows (39.45\% train, 48.99\% test duplicates); these duplicates arise from multiple candidate solutions per problem, with the train--test split enforced at the problem level (472 train / 119 test problems, zero crossover; Appendix~\ref{sec:dedup_stats}).

\subsection{Trace Representations}

We define seven conditions that vary the trace information available (Figure~\ref{fig:trace_overview}):
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{no\_trace}: Source code and test input only.
\item \texttt{full\_trace}: Complete line-by-line trace with variable states.
\item \texttt{VOTA} (Verdict-Optimized Trace Abstraction): Exception events + assertion-point comparisons + loop compression (Appendix~\ref{sec:vota_algorithm}).
\item \texttt{exception\_only}: Only exception/error events.
\item \texttt{ae\_only}: Only assertion-point actual/expected pairs.
\item \texttt{loop\_only}: Only loop compression summaries.
\item \texttt{VOTA\_no\_ae}: VOTA without assertion-point information.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_trace_overview}
\caption{Overview of the verdict prediction pipeline. Source code and a test input are executed to produce a full raw trace. Four conditions vary the trace information: \texttt{no\_trace} (75.13\%), \texttt{full\_trace} (81.44\%), VOTA (99.05\%), and \texttt{exception\_only} (99.83\%). Accuracies for Qwen-2.5-7B ($N{=}1{,}190$); multi-seed means where available (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}). Appendix~\ref{sec:vota_examples} provides input/output examples.}
\label{fig:trace_overview}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Models and Training}

We evaluate four models spanning three architecture families: \textbf{Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B/1.5B-Instruct} \citep{qwen2024coder}, \textbf{CodeLlama-13B-Instruct} \citep{roziere2024code}, and \textbf{DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Instruct} \citep{deepseek2024coder}, covering a 1.5B--13B parameter range (8.7$\times$).
All conditions use LoRA \citep{hu2022lora} (rank=16, $\alpha$=32, dropout=0.05) on all linear layers, 3 epochs, effective batch size 32, LR $2 \times 10^{-4}$ with linear warmup/decay, max sequence length 2{,}048 tokens.
Sequences exceeding this limit are truncated while preserving the verdict label.
The primary seed is 42; seeds 123 and 456 test \texttt{exception\_only} robustness (Appendix~\ref{sec:training_details}).

\subsection{Loss Ablation}

To probe the contribution of gradient dilution, we introduce \texttt{full\_trace\_label\_only}: identical full-trace input but loss computed only on the verdict token (trace positions masked).
TRL's SFTTrainer computes mean cross-entropy over label tokens, so label-only loss does not amplify the effective learning rate.
However, this ablation simultaneously changes three variables: (1)~which tokens receive gradient updates, (2)~the implicit regularization from predicting trace tokens, and (3)~the training signal distribution.
The resulting improvement over standard \texttt{full\_trace} provides a confounded upper bound on gradient dilution's contribution.
A cleaner test---masking 99.8\% of gradient updates during \texttt{exception\_only} training---matches the diagnostic token ratio of full traces while preserving compact input, isolating gradient dilution from content quality (\S\ref{sec:density}).

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{A descriptive two-pathway framework.}
The results suggest a descriptive two-pathway framework---not a causal model---for why complete traces degrade SFT learning.

\paragraph{Explicit pathway: diagnostic tokens.}
The diagnostic token ratio $d$ (\S2.1) correlates with accuracy at $r{=}0.949$ ($p{=}0.014$) across five conditions, and all four models achieve ${\geq}$99.33\% under \texttt{exception\_only} with zero variance on Qwen ($\sigma{=}$0.00, 3 seeds).
Although 55.5\% of failures are silent logic errors, \texttt{exception\_only} achieves 100\% fail recall because test assertions generate \texttt{AssertionError} events.
\texttt{ae\_only}, a strict superset, slightly underperforms \texttt{exception\_only} (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), confirming that additional content introduces noise.

\paragraph{Implicit pathway: terminal execution state.}
Tail-truncated traces achieve 93--97\% accuracy (Table~\ref{tab:tail_cross_model}) despite $d {\approx} 0$.
Tail truncation at ${\geq}$512 tokens captures \texttt{[Line~X]} variable values at final execution steps, which indirectly encode program outcome; below 384 tokens, the globals module dump consumes the entire budget, yielding ${\sim}$73\% ($\approx$ \texttt{no\_trace}).

\paragraph{Diagnostic signal dilution in complete traces.}
Both pathways' signals exist in full traces but are buried under thousands of intermediate tokens.
Controlled gradient masking confirms this: $-$0.84pp under 99.8\% mask limits gradient dilution to ${\leq}$5\% of the gap (\S\ref{sec:density}).
Figure~\ref{fig:training_dynamics} confirms: \texttt{exception\_only} converges within 50 steps (std\,=\,0 by step 200), while \texttt{full\_trace} oscillates (82--89\%) without convergence (final std\,=\,0.014); eval accuracy CV differs 9.4$\times$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:training_dynamics}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{training_dynamics}
\caption{Training dynamics: \texttt{exception\_only} converges to 99.83\% by step 150; \texttt{full\_trace} with label-only loss oscillates (82--89\%) without convergence.}
\label{fig:training_dynamics}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Why SFT over regex?}
SFT \texttt{exception\_only} (99.83\%) exceeds the best regex (B5, 99.33\%) by 0.50pp on caught-exception boundary cases (McNemar $p{=}.014$), provides calibrated probabilities (ECE\,=\,0.0027; \S\ref{sec:calibration}), and retains 97.65\% under marker removal vs.\ B5's 28.49\% (\S\ref{sec:nomarker}).
Bidirectional cross-dataset transfer (99.4--100\%) with near-majority \texttt{no\_trace} performance confirms the learned patterns are language-level, not dataset-specific.

\paragraph{The marginal value of complete traces.}
Source code alone achieves 73--76\% (\texttt{no\_trace}), so traces add at most ${\sim}$27pp; complete traces capture only 4--9pp of this headroom while exception events capture 24--27pp.
That more information degrades performance aligns with feature selection theory \citep{guyon2003introduction}, the Information Bottleneck \citep{tishby2000information}, and LIMA \citep{zhou2023lima}.

\paragraph{Design implications.}
The two-tier hierarchy suggests a practical fallback: extract exception events first; resort to tail-truncated state otherwise.
Tier~2 benefit is model-dependent (Qwen +10.9pp; CodeLlama +0.9pp), so trace design should match deployment model capacity.

